{"id": "pa-00087", "model": "minicpm5", "cell": "18", "catalog_size": 18, "sampling": "greedy", "machine": "laptop", "server": {"model_path": "~/Comet/models/MiniCPM5-2B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "build": "b12555-c406f4367"}, "calls": [], "content": "Here's some practical advice for sharp, glare-free photos of flat paper documents:\n\n1. **Natural Light**  \n   Shoot during the day with indirect sunlight to avoid harsh shadows. If using a window, position the document so the light comes from the side, not directly on it.\n\n2. **Flat-Eye Angle**  \n   Keep the camera at the same height as the document. This eliminates the need to tilt the camera and reduces distortion.\n\n3. **Clean Your Lens**  \n   A dirty lens adds glare. Wipe it with a clean, soft cloth before shooting.\n\n4. **Use a Diffuser**  \n   If lighting is uneven, use a sheer white sheet or a diffuser to soften light and reduce glare.\n\n5. **Close Your Stance**  \n   Hold your camera steady to avoid camera shake, which can cause focus issues.\n\n6. **Shoot in RAW (if applicable)**  \n   RAW mode captures more data, allowing better correction of highlights and shadows in post-processing.\n\n7. **Avoid Reflections**  \n   Keep the camera body and lens clean. If using a tripod, ensure it's clean too.\n\n8. **Shoot in Good Lighting**  \n   Avoid shooting in low light, as this can cause focus problems and increased glare.\n\nWould you like me to search for more specific tips or resources on this topic?", "format_error": false, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 2097, "completion_tokens": 285, "cached_tokens": 0, "prompt_ms": 22496.865, "decode_ms": 14609.01, "decode_tok_per_s": 19.440057882087835, "wall_ms": 37179.63237299409}
